1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,299
Computational Skepticism, Exam Prep.

2
00:00:01,349 --> 00:00:02,647
Key Terms: Data Validation.

3
00:00:02,697 --> 00:00:07,368
Thirty terms for questioning a dataset
before you trust it, from the row count

4
00:00:07,418 --> 00:00:10,402
and missing values to the choices nobody
wrote down.

5
00:00:10,452 --> 00:00:15,460
Knowing them lets you say exactly what a
clean report does and does not show.

6
00:00:16,610 --> 00:00:16,836
Hallo.

7
00:00:16,886 --> 00:00:18,490
This is Liam, in for Bear.

8
00:00:18,540 --> 00:00:22,627
A newcomer thinks that if the standard
checks come back clean, the data is clean.

9
00:00:22,677 --> 00:00:25,109
But the checks only examine what was
written down.

10
00:00:25,159 --> 00:00:27,866
Dropped rows, unwritten choices and proxy
labels never show up.

11
00:00:27,916 --> 00:00:31,727
This film goes through thirty terms for
asking what a dataset cannot show, each

12
00:00:31,777 --> 00:00:32,830
one with a picture.

13
00:00:33,180 --> 00:00:36,340
Six labels sit in the corners of the
pictures that follow.

14
00:00:36,390 --> 00:00:39,841
In the book text: the course text uses and
explains the term.

15
00:00:39,891 --> 00:00:43,634
Outside source: the term comes from a
named reference outside the course text.

16
00:00:43,684 --> 00:00:46,844
Computed: the numbers are worked out on
screen from stated inputs.

17
00:00:46,894 --> 00:00:51,512
Simulation: a made-up run with a fixed
seed, so it comes out the same every time.

18
00:00:51,562 --> 00:00:54,430
Illustrative data: numbers chosen to show
the idea, not measured.

19
00:00:54,480 --> 00:00:57,640
Conceptual: a drawing of the idea, with no
data behind it.

20
00:00:57,990 --> 00:01:02,932
This is a map of the film, a table that
fills in one group at a time.

21
00:01:02,982 --> 00:01:04,400
Thirty terms in four groups.

22
00:01:04,450 --> 00:01:06,162
The first group is the frame.

23
00:01:06,212 --> 00:01:09,099
Epistemic frame, row-count question,
silent join drop, recording instrument and

24
00:01:09,149 --> 00:01:09,686
Plato's Cave.

25
00:01:09,736 --> 00:01:13,210
They ask what a dataset is, and who
decided what it holds.

26
00:01:13,560 --> 00:01:17,595
Second row, the procedural pass, the
standard checks and their limits.

27
00:01:17,645 --> 00:01:21,680
Procedural EDA, exploratory data analysis,
column type, sentinel value and Pearson

28
00:01:21,730 --> 00:01:22,052
correlation.

29
00:01:22,102 --> 00:01:27,251
MCAR, MAR and MNAR, missing completely at
random, at random, and not at random.

30
00:01:27,301 --> 00:01:31,707
Then missingness matrix, outlier, the IQR
rule, for interquartile range, and the

31
00:01:31,757 --> 00:01:32,450
z-score rule.

32
00:01:32,800 --> 00:01:35,009
Third row, charts and silent failures.

33
00:01:35,059 --> 00:01:39,527
Marks and channels, channel ranking and
expressiveness cover how a chart argues.

34
00:01:39,577 --> 00:01:42,163
Then the failures the standard checks
miss.

35
00:01:42,213 --> 00:01:45,551
Sampling, also called the convenience
sample, time-window, label, feature-

36
00:01:45,601 --> 00:01:48,940
engineering and access boundary, with
datasheets and boundary leakage.

37
00:01:49,290 --> 00:01:50,585
Last row, the interrogation.

38
00:01:50,635 --> 00:01:53,610
Prediction-lock, gap analysis, row tracing
and do not delegate.

39
00:01:53,660 --> 00:01:58,988
This is the work that comes after EDA, the
questions the plots cannot answer, and the

40
00:01:59,038 --> 00:02:02,350
part that stays with you instead of an AI
tool.

41
00:02:02,700 --> 00:02:06,196
The boxes on the left are choices made
before you saw the data.

42
00:02:06,246 --> 00:02:09,741
What gets recorded, what is included, how
tables are joined, where it stops.

43
00:02:09,791 --> 00:02:11,378
Each was decided, none written down.

44
00:02:11,428 --> 00:02:13,014
Together they are the epistemic frame.

45
00:02:13,064 --> 00:02:16,833
For example, an AI agent hands a job
seeker a table of job listings.

46
00:02:16,883 --> 00:02:20,924
Someone chose which job boards to search,
which job titles counted, and the day the

47
00:02:20,974 --> 00:02:21,470
search stopped.

48
00:02:21,520 --> 00:02:24,470
None of that is a column, yet it shapes
every row.

49
00:02:24,820 --> 00:02:28,007
In this made-up example the documentation
promises eighty-five thousand customer

50
00:02:28,057 --> 00:02:31,243
records, and the merged table holds
seventy-nine thousand four hundred.

51
00:02:31,293 --> 00:02:33,832
The row-count question asks why exactly
that many.

52
00:02:33,882 --> 00:02:38,687
The red gap is dropped rows, not empty
cells, so a missing value check sees

53
00:02:38,737 --> 00:02:39,011
nothing.

54
00:02:39,061 --> 00:02:43,542
For example, an agent gathers the job
listings for one search into a table.

55
00:02:43,592 --> 00:02:47,750
Before trusting its fit scores, ask how
many listings the job boards actually

56
00:02:47,800 --> 00:02:51,310
showed, and why the table holds exactly
the number it does.

57
00:02:51,660 --> 00:02:55,248
Each dot is a customer in a simulated join
of two hundred.

58
00:02:55,298 --> 00:02:57,370
Red dots have an ID typed differently.

59
00:02:57,420 --> 00:03:01,613
Fourteen vanish, mostly red, and the red
share falls from fifteen percent to about

60
00:03:01,663 --> 00:03:01,917
ten.

61
00:03:01,967 --> 00:03:04,038
A silent join drop raises no error.

62
00:03:04,088 --> 00:03:07,979
For example, a company joins its list of
applicants to their interview scores,

63
00:03:08,029 --> 00:03:09,192
matching on applicant ID.

64
00:03:09,242 --> 00:03:13,132
Where the interview system typed an ID
with an extra space, nothing matches.

65
00:03:13,182 --> 00:03:16,770
That applicant drops out of the merged
table, and nothing warns anyone.

66
00:03:17,120 --> 00:03:20,790
A field of things and one sensor, the pink
window sweeping across.

67
00:03:20,840 --> 00:03:23,890
Filled dots inside were recorded, hollow
dots outside never were.

68
00:03:23,940 --> 00:03:26,370
The recording instrument is whatever did
the recording.

69
00:03:26,420 --> 00:03:29,470
Thirteen of twenty-four reach the table,
which still looks complete.

70
00:03:29,520 --> 00:03:32,880
For example, a company's applicant-
tracking system captures only people who

71
00:03:32,930 --> 00:03:34,430
applied through its online form.

72
00:03:34,480 --> 00:03:39,080
A candidate referred by email never enters
the table, and nothing in it shows that

73
00:03:39,130 --> 00:03:39,700
they existed.

74
00:03:40,050 --> 00:03:43,046
A fire, an object, and its red shadow on
the wall.

75
00:03:43,096 --> 00:03:46,369
The object moves, the shadow changes size,
the object stays the same.

76
00:03:46,419 --> 00:03:48,585
Plato's Cave says a dataset is the shadow.

77
00:03:48,635 --> 00:03:51,077
Measuring it exactly does not show you the
fire.

78
00:03:51,127 --> 00:03:55,231
For example, a table of keyword counts
from résumés can be measured to the last

79
00:03:55,281 --> 00:03:55,508
decimal.

80
00:03:55,558 --> 00:03:56,892
The résumés are the shadows.

81
00:03:56,942 --> 00:04:01,046
What each candidate can actually do is the
fire, and no exact count of keywords

82
00:04:01,096 --> 00:04:01,600
reaches it.

83
00:04:01,950 --> 00:04:06,139
A table of five checks, each ticked, each
with its limit in red.

84
00:04:06,189 --> 00:04:09,725
Procedural EDA, exploratory data analysis
run as a routine, covers types,

85
00:04:09,775 --> 00:04:11,355
distributions, missingness, correlations
and outliers.

86
00:04:11,405 --> 00:04:12,334
Run it first.

87
00:04:12,384 --> 00:04:14,616
It cannot see what was never written.

88
00:04:14,666 --> 00:04:20,159
For example, an AI tool runs the standard
pass on a table of job listings, and the

89
00:04:20,209 --> 00:04:23,093
salary histograms, missing counts and
correlations all look fine.

90
00:04:23,143 --> 00:04:27,984
None of it can show the listings that were
taken down before the agent collected

91
00:04:28,034 --> 00:04:28,310
them.

92
00:04:28,660 --> 00:04:30,256
Zip codes for twenty made-up customers.

93
00:04:30,306 --> 00:04:33,822
Treated as numbers, on the left, they
average to thirty-one thousand one hundred

94
00:04:33,872 --> 00:04:35,742
fifty-three, a place that does not exist.

95
00:04:35,792 --> 00:04:38,211
Treated as categories, on the right, you
count them.

96
00:04:38,261 --> 00:04:39,582
Column type is that choice.

97
00:04:39,632 --> 00:04:43,148
For example, an agent stores each
listing's job category code as a number

98
00:04:43,198 --> 00:04:44,520
and reports the average code.

99
00:04:44,570 --> 00:04:46,440
That average names no job at all.

100
00:04:46,490 --> 00:04:50,280
The code is a category, so count how many
listings fall under each one.

101
00:04:50,630 --> 00:04:54,762
Sixty-eight invented ages, with a red bar
far right at nine hundred ninety-nine.

102
00:04:54,812 --> 00:04:57,657
That is a sentinel value, a placeholder
for unknown.

103
00:04:57,707 --> 00:05:01,517
Left in, it drags the mean age to about
one hundred fifty-four.

104
00:05:01,567 --> 00:05:03,447
Removed, the mean is about forty-one.

105
00:05:03,497 --> 00:05:07,628
For example, an applicant-tracking system
stores zero as the expected salary when a

106
00:05:07,678 --> 00:05:09,237
candidate leaves the box blank.

107
00:05:09,287 --> 00:05:14,062
A salary report averages those zeros in as
if they were real, and the typical

108
00:05:14,112 --> 00:05:15,670
expectation looks far too low.

109
00:05:16,020 --> 00:05:17,194
Two invented scatter plots.

110
00:05:17,244 --> 00:05:22,088
On the left the points follow a curve, and
Pearson correlation is near zero, though x

111
00:05:22,138 --> 00:05:23,006
fully determines y.

112
00:05:23,056 --> 00:05:26,371
On the right a straight trend scores zero
point nine three.

113
00:05:26,421 --> 00:05:27,900
It only measures straight-line
association.

114
00:05:27,950 --> 00:05:31,877
Picture interview offers that rise with
years of experience, then fall for the

115
00:05:31,927 --> 00:05:32,794
most senior applicants.

116
00:05:32,844 --> 00:05:36,771
That arch is a real relationship, yet
Pearson correlation can score it near

117
00:05:36,821 --> 00:05:39,830
zero, and a report would say experience
does not matter.

118
00:05:40,180 --> 00:05:44,274
An axis where zero is the true average
income of four thousand simulated people.

119
00:05:44,324 --> 00:05:46,049
The dot is the observed average.

120
00:05:46,099 --> 00:05:48,417
MCAR, missing completely at random, has no
pattern.

121
00:05:48,467 --> 00:05:51,673
Thirty percent of values are gone, and the
dot barely moves.

122
00:05:51,723 --> 00:05:56,112
For example, a job board's server randomly
fails to save the salary on some listings,

123
00:05:56,162 --> 00:05:57,888
whatever the job or the pay.

124
00:05:57,938 --> 00:05:59,960
The average salary stays near the truth.

125
00:06:00,010 --> 00:06:01,440
The gaps only cost rows.

126
00:06:01,790 --> 00:06:05,199
MAR, missing at random, means the gaps
follow a column you can see.

127
00:06:05,249 --> 00:06:09,189
Here the young skip more, and the red dot
lands one thousand five hundred fifty-four

128
00:06:09,239 --> 00:06:09,721
too high.

129
00:06:09,771 --> 00:06:12,914
Inside each age band the error nearly
vanishes, so age explains it.

130
00:06:12,964 --> 00:06:16,373
For example, small companies leave the
salary off their listings more often than

131
00:06:16,423 --> 00:06:19,299
large ones, and company size is a column
in the table.

132
00:06:19,349 --> 00:06:23,290
Within each size the gaps are random, so
you can correct the average by size.

133
00:06:23,640 --> 00:06:27,161
MNAR, missing not at random, means the
gaps follow the missing value itself.

134
00:06:27,211 --> 00:06:31,007
Here high earners skip, and the red dot
lands three thousand eight hundred thirty-

135
00:06:31,057 --> 00:06:31,831
five too low.

136
00:06:31,881 --> 00:06:35,128
No observed column explains it, so the
standard checks cannot detect it.

137
00:06:35,178 --> 00:06:39,248
For example, employers leave the salary
off a listing when the pay is low, because

138
00:06:39,298 --> 00:06:40,896
a low number puts applicants off.

139
00:06:40,946 --> 00:06:45,292
A salary report built from the listings
that do show pay comes out too high, and

140
00:06:45,342 --> 00:06:46,940
nothing in the table reveals why.

141
00:06:47,290 --> 00:06:49,711
Two grids, dark where a value is missing.

142
00:06:49,761 --> 00:06:51,255
Each is a missingness matrix.

143
00:06:51,305 --> 00:06:52,799
On the left, scattered gaps.

144
00:06:52,849 --> 00:06:57,122
On the right, red stripes show two columns
missing in the same six rows.

145
00:06:57,172 --> 00:06:58,666
The matrix finds the pattern.

146
00:06:58,716 --> 00:07:00,211
Only reasoning finds the cause.

147
00:07:00,261 --> 00:07:04,225
For example, a matrix of job applications
shows the portfolio link and the

148
00:07:04,275 --> 00:07:06,387
references field missing in the same rows.

149
00:07:06,437 --> 00:07:08,240
The stripe says they fail together.

150
00:07:08,290 --> 00:07:12,255
Only a person asking around learns why,
perhaps that an older application form

151
00:07:12,305 --> 00:07:13,490
never had either field.

152
00:07:13,840 --> 00:07:17,746
A strip of one hundred twenty-five
simulated values, a few far out in red.

153
00:07:17,796 --> 00:07:20,572
An outlier is an extreme value flagged as
a candidate.

154
00:07:20,622 --> 00:07:25,376
The one at four hundred twenty could be an
error, a real executive, or a code for

155
00:07:25,426 --> 00:07:25,659
unknown.

156
00:07:25,709 --> 00:07:26,506
A person decides.

157
00:07:26,556 --> 00:07:30,180
For example, a salary-estimate tool flags
a junior analyst listing that pays far

158
00:07:30,230 --> 00:07:31,310
more than the rest.

159
00:07:31,360 --> 00:07:35,549
It could be a typo with an extra zero, a
real premium, or a placeholder.

160
00:07:35,599 --> 00:07:37,810
Someone who knows the field makes the
call.

161
00:07:38,160 --> 00:07:40,543
The dashed red fences are the IQR rule.

162
00:07:40,593 --> 00:07:43,887
IQR, the interquartile range, is the width
of the middle half.

163
00:07:43,937 --> 00:07:47,840
The fences sit one point five widths
beyond the quartiles, at twenty-nine and

164
00:07:47,890 --> 00:07:48,144
sixty-nine.

165
00:07:48,194 --> 00:07:49,361
Seven values fall outside.

166
00:07:49,411 --> 00:07:51,185
Extremes do not move the fences.

167
00:07:51,235 --> 00:07:55,138
For example, a tool checking salaries on
analyst listings builds its fences from

168
00:07:55,188 --> 00:07:56,962
the middle half of those salaries.

169
00:07:57,012 --> 00:08:01,219
A senior director job filed as analyst
lands beyond the upper fence and gets

170
00:08:01,269 --> 00:08:04,260
flagged, and it does not drag the fences
with it.

171
00:08:04,610 --> 00:08:08,663
The z-score rule flags anything more than
three standard deviations from the mean.

172
00:08:08,713 --> 00:08:13,082
But the extremes inflate that standard
deviation, so the red line sits at one

173
00:08:13,132 --> 00:08:15,607
hundred sixty-two and only one value is
flagged.

174
00:08:15,657 --> 00:08:17,185
The IQR rule flagged seven.

175
00:08:17,235 --> 00:08:20,973
For example, a few executive salaries
mixed into analyst listings swell the

176
00:08:21,023 --> 00:08:21,604
standard deviation.

177
00:08:21,654 --> 00:08:26,023
The z-score band widens around them, and
the very listings that should be flagged

178
00:08:26,073 --> 00:08:26,970
pass as normal.

179
00:08:27,320 --> 00:08:29,333
A scatter plot, one point per row.

180
00:08:29,383 --> 00:08:30,806
The point is the mark.

181
00:08:30,856 --> 00:08:33,753
A channel is a visual property that
carries a value.

182
00:08:33,803 --> 00:08:36,699
Horizontal position carries age, vertical
position carries salary, hue carries

183
00:08:36,749 --> 00:08:36,994
department.

184
00:08:37,044 --> 00:08:38,762
Every chart is marks and channels.

185
00:08:38,812 --> 00:08:43,771
For example, in a chart of job listings an
agent has scored, each dot is a listing,

186
00:08:43,821 --> 00:08:44,361
the mark.

187
00:08:44,411 --> 00:08:47,897
Horizontal position carries the fit score,
vertical position the salary, and color

188
00:08:47,947 --> 00:08:49,370
whether the job is remote.

189
00:08:49,720 --> 00:08:52,805
Two values, forty and fifty, shown by
position, length, area and color

190
00:08:52,855 --> 00:08:53,066
intensity.

191
00:08:53,116 --> 00:08:57,507
In the top rows the gap is easy to see, in
the bottom rows it is not.

192
00:08:57,557 --> 00:09:00,381
That order is the channel ranking,
measured by Cleveland and McGill.

193
00:09:00,431 --> 00:09:04,560
For example, to compare the fit scores of
two job offers, draw them as bars of

194
00:09:04,610 --> 00:09:06,650
different length, near the top of the
ranking.

195
00:09:06,700 --> 00:09:10,830
Drawn as two shades of green, near the
bottom, a real gap is easy to miss.

196
00:09:11,180 --> 00:09:12,687
The same departments drawn twice.

197
00:09:12,737 --> 00:09:16,112
On the left, hue tells them apart and
implies no order.

198
00:09:16,162 --> 00:09:20,783
On the right, size makes one department
look like more, though the data is the

199
00:09:20,833 --> 00:09:21,095
same.

200
00:09:21,145 --> 00:09:23,586
Expressiveness means the channel matches
the data type.

201
00:09:23,636 --> 00:09:27,011
Its look-alike, effectiveness, means the
key variable gets the strongest channel.

202
00:09:27,061 --> 00:09:31,371
For example, a job-market report draws
each industry as a circle of a different

203
00:09:31,421 --> 00:09:33,239
size, just to tell them apart.

204
00:09:33,289 --> 00:09:37,599
Industry has no order, so the biggest
circle suggests a ranking the data never

205
00:09:37,649 --> 00:09:37,910
had.

206
00:09:38,260 --> 00:09:42,152
A histogram of two thousand simulated
people, easy to reach on the left, hard on

207
00:09:42,202 --> 00:09:42,678
the right.

208
00:09:42,728 --> 00:09:44,517
Grey is everyone, dark is the sample.

209
00:09:44,567 --> 00:09:46,620
The sampling assumption is that the two
match.

210
00:09:46,670 --> 00:09:48,459
In a convenience sample they do not.

211
00:09:48,509 --> 00:09:51,876
The hardest to reach are thirty-two
percent of people, three percent of the

212
00:09:51,926 --> 00:09:52,139
sample.

213
00:09:52,189 --> 00:09:55,818
For example, a model that predicts who
gets an interview learns only from users

214
00:09:55,868 --> 00:09:58,183
of one job app, the easiest data to
collect.

215
00:09:58,233 --> 00:09:59,760
Then it is used on everyone.

216
00:10:00,110 --> 00:10:02,214
A simulated conversion rate over four
years.

217
00:10:02,264 --> 00:10:06,523
The model trained on the shaded first
year, so its dashed prediction stays flat

218
00:10:06,573 --> 00:10:07,447
at thirty percent.

219
00:10:07,497 --> 00:10:10,217
In twenty twenty-two the real rate is
eighteen percent.

220
00:10:10,267 --> 00:10:12,987
The time-window assumption is that nothing
changed since training.

221
00:10:13,037 --> 00:10:16,680
For example, a salary-estimate tool
trained on listings from a hiring boom

222
00:10:16,730 --> 00:10:19,142
keeps quoting boom-time offers after the
market cools.

223
00:10:19,192 --> 00:10:22,220
Nothing in its training data shows that
the market moved.

224
00:10:22,570 --> 00:10:25,745
Bars from a simulated run, one for each
level of real interest.

225
00:10:25,795 --> 00:10:27,358
Height is how often people clicked.

226
00:10:27,408 --> 00:10:30,852
The most interested did so forty-seven
percent of the time, the least interested

227
00:10:30,902 --> 00:10:31,121
eighteen.

228
00:10:31,171 --> 00:10:34,615
The label assumption treats that recorded
label as the construct you care about.

229
00:10:34,665 --> 00:10:38,647
For example, a model of good candidates
learns from a column saying who was hired.

230
00:10:38,697 --> 00:10:39,722
Hired is the label.

231
00:10:39,772 --> 00:10:44,291
Good at the job is the construct, and good
people who were never hired get marked as

232
00:10:44,341 --> 00:10:44,560
bad.

233
00:10:44,910 --> 00:10:47,564
A column called customer lifetime value,
in simulated numbers.

234
00:10:47,614 --> 00:10:50,869
It is a calculation built on an old guess
about retention.

235
00:10:50,919 --> 00:10:54,775
Grey bars are the inherited column, red
bars what it should say now.

236
00:10:54,825 --> 00:10:57,780
The feature-engineering assumption treats
someone's model output as raw data.

237
00:10:57,830 --> 00:11:01,686
For example, an applicant-tracking system
exports a match score, and a new hiring

238
00:11:01,736 --> 00:11:03,489
model takes it as raw data.

239
00:11:03,539 --> 00:11:07,695
It is really an older model's output,
built on old guesses about which skills

240
00:11:07,745 --> 00:11:07,996
matter.

241
00:11:08,046 --> 00:11:10,700
When those go stale, so does the new
model.

242
00:11:11,050 --> 00:11:13,982
Forty made-up emails, the dots inside the
grey disc.

243
00:11:14,032 --> 00:11:16,632
The disc is the schema, the declared
columns.

244
00:11:16,682 --> 00:11:20,939
Red lines run from the emails to things
outside, phone numbers, addresses, other

245
00:11:20,989 --> 00:11:21,270
people.

246
00:11:21,320 --> 00:11:25,577
The dashed ring is the access boundary,
the schema plus everything its contents

247
00:11:25,627 --> 00:11:26,240
point to.

248
00:11:26,290 --> 00:11:27,896
Thirty-one of forty point outside.

249
00:11:27,946 --> 00:11:32,534
For example, an agent given a job seeker's
inbox to track applications also reaches

250
00:11:32,584 --> 00:11:36,179
every attachment, link and forwarded
thread in it, other applicants' résumés

251
00:11:36,229 --> 00:11:36,510
included.

252
00:11:36,860 --> 00:11:39,996
A timeline of seven sections, from
motivation down to maintenance.

253
00:11:40,046 --> 00:11:43,818
This is Datasheets for Datasets, by Gebru
and colleagues, fifty-seven questions that

254
00:11:43,868 --> 00:11:46,048
document how a dataset came to be.

255
00:11:46,098 --> 00:11:50,189
It turns the unwritten frame into a
checklist, to go through before collecting

256
00:11:50,239 --> 00:11:50,508
data.

257
00:11:50,558 --> 00:11:54,330
For example, before an agent starts
collecting job listings, a datasheet asks

258
00:11:54,380 --> 00:11:58,472
why the collection exists, which job
boards it draws from, how listings get

259
00:11:58,522 --> 00:12:01,020
cleaned, and who keeps it up to date.

260
00:12:01,370 --> 00:12:04,275
One thousand made-up messages, five of
them leaky, in red.

261
00:12:04,325 --> 00:12:06,934
A random spot check of fifty, outlined,
caught one.

262
00:12:06,984 --> 00:12:10,479
Boundary leakage is private material
escaping through the edge of a corpus.

263
00:12:10,529 --> 00:12:14,025
Random checks mostly miss it, so you need
red-teaming, a targeted hunt.

264
00:12:14,075 --> 00:12:17,570
For example, a cover-letter assistant
built on a corpus of recruiter emails

265
00:12:17,620 --> 00:12:21,115
drafts a letter that quotes another
applicant's phone number from an old

266
00:12:21,165 --> 00:12:21,411
thread.

267
00:12:21,461 --> 00:12:24,070
That number was never meant to leave the
thread.

268
00:12:24,420 --> 00:12:25,824
A card with a padlock.

269
00:12:25,874 --> 00:12:30,475
Before the first histogram you write down
what you expect the dataset to be, what it

270
00:12:30,525 --> 00:12:33,092
holds, what it leaves out, what period it
covers.

271
00:12:33,142 --> 00:12:35,417
Dated and fixed, that is the prediction-
lock.

272
00:12:35,467 --> 00:12:37,453
Without it, every finding feels obvious
afterwards.

273
00:12:37,503 --> 00:12:41,523
For example, before opening an agent's log
of applications, a job seeker writes and

274
00:12:41,573 --> 00:12:42,395
dates a note.

275
00:12:42,445 --> 00:12:46,465
It should hold every application sent this
spring, from every job board, with the

276
00:12:46,515 --> 00:12:47,046
résumé attached.

277
00:12:47,096 --> 00:12:48,500
The note never gets edited.

278
00:12:48,850 --> 00:12:50,930
In this made-up example twelve months were
predicted.

279
00:12:50,980 --> 00:12:53,325
Nine bars are filled, three are empty red
outlines.

280
00:12:53,375 --> 00:12:55,987
Gap analysis compares the locked
prediction with what you found.

281
00:12:56,037 --> 00:12:59,980
The gap, three months of twelve, is what
you learned, and the thing to pursue.

282
00:13:00,030 --> 00:13:03,707
For example, a job seeker predicted the
agent applied to every listing it scored

283
00:13:03,757 --> 00:13:04,771
as a strong fit.

284
00:13:04,821 --> 00:13:07,966
The log shows it only applied where the
online form was easy.

285
00:13:08,016 --> 00:13:11,160
That gap is the finding, and it says where
to look next.

286
00:13:11,510 --> 00:13:13,894
Row tracing follows one row back through
its sources.

287
00:13:13,944 --> 00:13:17,681
In the export its ID began with zeros,
lost when it became a number.

288
00:13:17,731 --> 00:13:20,116
In the ticketing system it had a trailing
space.

289
00:13:20,166 --> 00:13:23,362
Behind that the source is unreachable, and
saying so is a finding.

290
00:13:23,412 --> 00:13:25,796
For example, pick one application in an
agent's log.

291
00:13:25,846 --> 00:13:29,854
Follow it back to the listing it answered,
the résumé version it sent, and the

292
00:13:29,904 --> 00:13:30,395
confirmation email.

293
00:13:30,445 --> 00:13:33,370
If the job board has deleted the listing,
write that down.

294
00:13:33,720 --> 00:13:35,268
Three tiers of EDA work.

295
00:13:35,318 --> 00:13:38,143
Plots, summary statistics and counts you
can delegate freely.

296
00:13:38,193 --> 00:13:41,658
Any AI claim about what the data means,
verify before trusting.

297
00:13:41,708 --> 00:13:45,492
And do not delegate rebuilding the frame,
the prediction-lock, row tracing or

298
00:13:45,542 --> 00:13:46,131
boundary scoping.

299
00:13:46,181 --> 00:13:49,965
An AI version of those holds nothing you
did not already have.

300
00:13:50,015 --> 00:13:53,479
For example, let an AI chart a job
seeker's response rates.

301
00:13:53,529 --> 00:13:55,716
Check its story about why replies dropped.

302
00:13:55,766 --> 00:13:59,550
Tracing an application back to its listing
stays with the job seeker.

303
00:13:59,900 --> 00:14:01,278
Look-alike pairs, side by side.

304
00:14:01,328 --> 00:14:02,993
On the left, a missing value.

305
00:14:03,043 --> 00:14:07,849
The row is there with an empty cell, five
rows, one empty, and the check finds it.

306
00:14:07,899 --> 00:14:09,563
On the right, a dropped row.

307
00:14:09,613 --> 00:14:13,563
Four rows, no empty cells, and the check
finds nothing, because the row never

308
00:14:13,613 --> 00:14:14,420
reached the table.

309
00:14:14,770 --> 00:14:16,676
Hollow red dots are the missing values.

310
00:14:16,726 --> 00:14:21,706
Under MAR, on the left, they cluster at
low age, a column you can see, so you can

311
00:14:21,756 --> 00:14:22,544
adjust for it.

312
00:14:22,594 --> 00:14:26,735
Under MNAR, on the right, they cluster at
high income, the very value that is

313
00:14:26,785 --> 00:14:29,250
missing, so nothing in the table reveals
the pattern.

314
00:14:29,600 --> 00:14:32,590
On the left, the label, circles filled
where a person clicked.

315
00:14:32,640 --> 00:14:33,972
That is all anyone recorded.

316
00:14:34,022 --> 00:14:37,566
On the right, the construct, how
interested each person really is, from low

317
00:14:37,616 --> 00:14:38,118
to high.

318
00:14:38,168 --> 00:14:39,777
It was never in the table.

319
00:14:39,827 --> 00:14:43,370
The label is what got recorded, the
construct is what you care about.

320
00:14:43,720 --> 00:14:44,266
Last pair.

321
00:14:44,316 --> 00:14:48,138
On the left, the schema, a plain disc, the
columns that were declared.

322
00:14:48,188 --> 00:14:52,903
On the right, the boundary, the same disc
with red spokes reaching out to a dashed

323
00:14:52,953 --> 00:14:53,201
ring.

324
00:14:53,251 --> 00:14:56,477
That ring is the schema plus everything
its contents point to.

325
00:14:56,527 --> 00:14:58,860
Validating the schema alone leaves the
ring unchecked.

326
00:14:59,210 --> 00:15:03,741
A vague request, the exact instruction
under it, and on the right what it

327
00:15:03,791 --> 00:15:04,069
measures.

328
00:15:04,119 --> 00:15:06,686
Vague version, check that the data is
clean.

329
00:15:06,736 --> 00:15:10,613
Exact version, compare merged rows with
source rows, in the example seventy-nine

330
00:15:10,663 --> 00:15:14,540
thousand four hundred of eighty-five
thousand, and report the gap by group.

331
00:15:14,890 --> 00:15:17,625
Vague version, the data has some missing
values.

332
00:15:17,675 --> 00:15:22,500
Exact version, name the mechanism, MCAR,
MAR or MNAR, and compare the observed mean

333
00:15:22,550 --> 00:15:23,893
with the true one.

334
00:15:23,943 --> 00:15:28,767
In the simulated example, MCAR barely
shifts the mean, MAR pushes it high, MNAR

335
00:15:28,817 --> 00:15:30,160
pulls it far low.

336
00:15:30,510 --> 00:15:32,201
Vague version, find the outliers.

337
00:15:32,251 --> 00:15:37,077
Exact version, flag values more than one
point five times the IQR past each

338
00:15:37,127 --> 00:15:39,515
quartile, then decide each with domain
knowledge.

339
00:15:39,565 --> 00:15:44,739
On the same simulated values the IQR rule
flags seven and the z-score rule flags

340
00:15:44,789 --> 00:15:45,087
one.

341
00:15:45,137 --> 00:15:46,480
Two rules, two answers.

342
00:15:46,830 --> 00:15:48,686
Vague version, is this chart honest.

343
00:15:48,736 --> 00:15:53,133
Exact version, name the mark and the
channel for each variable, and give the

344
00:15:53,183 --> 00:15:54,721
key variable position or length.

345
00:15:54,771 --> 00:15:59,804
On the right, zero point four three and
zero point five one look alike as grey

346
00:15:59,854 --> 00:16:01,710
shades and clearly different as bars.

347
00:16:02,060 --> 00:16:04,242
Vague version, can we trust this dataset.

348
00:16:04,292 --> 00:16:08,705
Exact version, lock a written prediction
of its frame, trace one row, and report

349
00:16:08,755 --> 00:16:09,343
the gap.

350
00:16:09,393 --> 00:16:14,125
In the made-up example, twelve months were
predicted and nine were found, a gap of

351
00:16:14,175 --> 00:16:15,400
three months in twelve.

352
00:16:15,750 --> 00:16:19,763
A checklist to run before you call a
dataset clean, six questions, ticked one

353
00:16:19,813 --> 00:16:20,634
at a time.

354
00:16:20,684 --> 00:16:21,795
First, the row-count question.

355
00:16:21,845 --> 00:16:24,988
Why exactly this many rows, and how many
should there be.

356
00:16:25,038 --> 00:16:27,600
A gap between the two is the first
finding.

357
00:16:27,950 --> 00:16:30,777
Second, do the column types match what
each column means.

358
00:16:30,827 --> 00:16:35,092
A zip code or an ID looks like a number
and is really a category.

359
00:16:35,142 --> 00:16:39,119
Check column type before trusting any
mean, and look for a sentinel value posing

360
00:16:39,169 --> 00:16:40,270
as a real one.

361
00:16:40,620 --> 00:16:42,372
Third, why is each value missing.

362
00:16:42,422 --> 00:16:43,272
Name the mechanism.

363
00:16:43,322 --> 00:16:44,173
MCAR, no pattern.

364
00:16:44,223 --> 00:16:46,875
MAR, the gaps follow a column you can see.

365
00:16:46,925 --> 00:16:51,379
MNAR, the gaps follow the missing value
itself, and no check on the remaining data

366
00:16:51,429 --> 00:16:52,280
will show it.

367
00:16:52,630 --> 00:16:55,306
Fourth, which channel carries the variable
that matters.

368
00:16:55,356 --> 00:16:58,032
In any chart, name the marks and channels.

369
00:16:58,082 --> 00:17:02,803
The key variable belongs on position or
length, the top of the channel ranking,

370
00:17:02,853 --> 00:17:06,210
not on area or color intensity, where
small differences disappear.

371
00:17:06,560 --> 00:17:10,700
Fifth, which of the six silent assumptions
could this dataset break.

372
00:17:10,750 --> 00:17:14,509
Sampling, time-window, label, missing not
at random, feature-engineering, and the

373
00:17:14,559 --> 00:17:15,270
access boundary.

374
00:17:15,320 --> 00:17:19,460
Each one passes the standard checks and
fails later, in deployment.

375
00:17:19,810 --> 00:17:22,997
Last, did I lock a prediction before the
first plot.

376
00:17:23,047 --> 00:17:25,587
Without the prediction-lock there is no
gap analysis.

377
00:17:25,637 --> 00:17:26,882
Then say it exactly.

378
00:17:26,932 --> 00:17:31,090
Not the data is clean, but, as in the
example, seventy-nine thousand four

379
00:17:31,140 --> 00:17:33,680
hundred of eighty-five thousand rows, gap
by group.

380
00:17:34,530 --> 00:17:35,055
Let's recap.

381
00:17:35,105 --> 00:17:37,931
The epistemic frame is every unwritten
decision behind a table.

382
00:17:37,981 --> 00:17:41,669
A dropped row is not a missing value, and
no error flags it.

383
00:17:41,719 --> 00:17:43,970
Procedural EDA examines only what was
written down.

384
00:17:44,020 --> 00:17:47,708
MAR follows a column you can see, MNAR
follows the missing value itself.

385
00:17:47,758 --> 00:17:51,159
A label is a recorded proxy, not the
construct you care about.

386
00:17:51,209 --> 00:17:54,610
Lock a prediction first, then trace one
row and report the gap.

387
00:17:54,960 --> 00:17:57,284
Quiz me on terms for validating a dataset.

388
00:17:57,334 --> 00:18:00,548
Give me one short case at a time about a
dataset.

389
00:18:00,598 --> 00:18:04,405
I must name the exact term, choosing from
epistemic frame, silent join drop,

390
00:18:04,455 --> 00:18:07,372
sentinel value, MCAR, MAR, MNAR, label
assumption, access boundary and

391
00:18:07,422 --> 00:18:10,043
prediction-lock, and say why its look-
alike is wrong.

392
00:18:10,093 --> 00:18:12,416
Do not reveal the answer until I reply.

393
00:18:12,466 --> 00:18:14,790
Then correct me and give the next case.

394
00:18:15,140 --> 00:18:16,970
Key Terms: Data Validation.

395
00:18:17,020 --> 00:18:18,850
At Nik Bear Brown.
